// ============================================================================
//  flap_init.rules -- give the flap sliders a position instead of NaN
// ============================================================================
//  GENERATED by make_generated_rules.py -- edit that, not this file.
//
//  THE PROBLEM
//  -----------
//  The HABPanel flap pages bind their slider to FlapActuator_N_Target.  That
//  Item is only ever written by an operator dragging the slider: it sits in
//  gPersist_On_Change_1minute, which has NO restoreOnStartup, and on OH5 its
//  MQTT channel is commented out entirely (read-only mode).  So after every
//  restart all 18 Targets are NULL, and a slider bound to NULL renders as
//  "NaNmm" pinned to the bottom of its travel -- on all eighteen at once.
//
//  THE FIX
//  -------
//  When a Target is NULL, seed it from what the potentiometer actually reports
//  (FlapActuator_N_Current), so the slider opens where the flap really is.
//
//  postUpdate, NEVER sendCommand.  postUpdate sets openHAB's own state and puts
//  the slider in the right place; sendCommand would go out on cmnd/ and order a
//  real actuator to move.  On OH5 that is inert today, but this same file runs
//  on production where those channels are live, and seeding must never drive
//  anything.
//
//  ONLY when the reading is a plausible position, 0..200 mm.  Pots do report
//  impossible values (-51, 274, 490), and units do go offline.  Inventing a
//  position for those would put a confident number under a flap nobody can see
//  -- worse than an obviously broken display.
//  Those sliders stay NaN, and the redesigned tile above them says so in red.
//
//  Runs at startup, at season start, AND on the minute tick -- the last one so
//  that a flap which comes back online has its slider populated within a minute
//  instead of at the next restart.  It only ever acts on an unset Target, so it
//  can never overwrite an operator's setting.
//
//  THE STARTUP NAME-RESOLUTION ERROR -- BENIGN, SELF-HEALING, AND NOT WORTH A THIRD FIX
//  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  On openHAB 3.1 the DSL interpreter intermittently fails to resolve a name on this
//  rule's FIRST execution after a restart.  Three production restarts on 2026-08-20
//  each showed it, on a DIFFERENT name every time:
//
//      The name 'NULL' cannot be resolved to an item or type;      line 47
//      The name 'FlapActuator_4_Current' cannot be resolved ...;   line 114
//      The name 'FlapActuator_1_Current' cannot be resolved ...;   line 82
//
//  The bytes on those lines are plain ASCII and identical in shape to lines that
//  resolve fine elsewhere in the same config, and the next minute tick always runs
//  clean and seeds every flap.  It is the interpreter under startup load, not a defect
//  in this file.  OH5 (5.1.4) has never shown it in any restart.
//
//  TWO FIXES WERE TRIED HERE AND NEITHER WORKS.  Do not try a third without new
//  evidence -- the cause is in the interpreter, not in anything this file can say:
//
//    1. Swapping `== NULL || == UNDEF` for `!(... instanceof DecimalType)`.  All that
//       did was move which name failed first, from the NULL literal to an Item.  (The
//       instanceof form was kept anyway: it is exactly equivalent for a plain Number
//       Item, and it is the same check used one line further in.)
//
//    2. The try/catch around each flap below.  It does NOT catch this.  Verified on
//       production 2026-08-20 10:19: the failing reference sat two lines inside flap 1's
//       own `try` block (lines 80/82 as the file stood then, before this note was added)
//       and the error still aborted the whole rule.  openHAB
//       raises this by aborting script evaluation, not by throwing a Java exception
//       into the script's own frame, so a DSL catch clause never sees it.
//
//  The try/catch is kept because it is still correct defensive structure for ordinary
//  exceptions -- a bad state conversion in one flap should not cost the other
//  seventeen -- and `deferred` counts those.  It will read 0 for the startup error.
//
//  WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENS, AND WHY IT IS ACCEPTABLE: the rule aborts part-way, so some
//  flaps are seeded and the rest wait.  The next minute tick seeds them.  Net cost is
//  one ERROR line and some sliders arriving a minute late, once per restart, on a
//  system that restarts rarely.  Seeding is not safety-critical -- it stops a slider
//  reading "NaNmm", nothing more.
//
//  NOTE the same `== NULL` form is still used in on_startup / season_start /
//  season_end / temperature_alarm.  Those have never been seen to fail and are not all
//  generated, so they were left alone deliberately.
// ============================================================================

rule "Flap sliders - seed target from the reported position"
when
    Item SystemStarting changed to OFF or
    Item seasonStart received command ON or
    Item oneMinuteTriggerSwitch changed
then
    var int seeded   = 0
    var int blind    = 0
    var int deferred = 0

    try {
        if(!(FlapActuator_1_Target.state instanceof DecimalType)) {
            if(FlapActuator_1_Current.state instanceof DecimalType) {
                val double p1 = (FlapActuator_1_Current.state as DecimalType).doubleValue
                if(p1 >= 0.0 && p1 <= 200.0) {
                    FlapActuator_1_Target.postUpdate(p1)
                    seeded = seeded + 1
                } else {
                    blind = blind + 1
                }
            } else {
                blind = blind + 1
            }
        }
    } catch(Throwable tx1) {
        deferred = deferred + 1
    }
    try {
        if(!(FlapActuator_2_Target.state instanceof DecimalType)) {
            if(FlapActuator_2_Current.state instanceof DecimalType) {
